WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.490
Film Courage: What comes first the budget
or the script?

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:04.910
John Paul Rice, movie producer: Script.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:07.069
The script dictates the budget.

00:00:07.069 --> 00:00:12.860
The script is your heart and soul, the spine
of the whole thing that you’re going to

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:19.400
build off of the foundation of everything
that you’re going to lay on top of it and

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:25.420
if you have money first and you’re writing
to the budget of that, I think your creativity

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.349
is paramount.

00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:36.640
And the dealing within the realities of what
resources are available to you and then you

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.629
start talking about money and how you’re
going to fulfill that vision but if the vision

00:00:41.629 --> 00:00:47.050
is coming from a monetary...me personally...I’ve
had more money on some of my other films,

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.110
didn’t mean I did a better job producing.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:54.300
In fact my third film MOTHER’S RED DRESS,
I had more money than any of the other ones

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.450
and it was my worst job producing/

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:58.489
Film Courage: Why?

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:03.850
John: Because I thought that by having more
money to pay more money to each position and

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:11.320
person that was working on it I was just going
to get better talent and therefore I took

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:17.200
my better part of myself out of the equation
just that it was going to be solved by money.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.490
And I learned a very valuable lesson on that.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:26.460
We got the film done and everything was great
and it worked out fine but I saw dollars and

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:32.430
that I could get this kind of DP and it didn’t
translate into success for the project.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:37.790
In fact the person that I brought on once
that DP left the project for another project

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:44.200
in the middle of shooting, I brought in somebody
else that had less equipment, less experience

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:49.190
and he did a fantastic job because his motivation
was correct.

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:53.770
So if you look at it from this standpoint,
if you have money and you’re going to pay

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:55.000
people right?

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:01.090
We all want to get paid and we’ve got to
work to live and all that but if that is the

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:05.229
focus first then I think that you’re doing
the opposite.

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:11.080
It’s like Okay we’ve got a marketing campaign
we can do now let’s find a script if that’s

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:14.489
the kind of content that we want to sell as
opposed to what comes from within.

00:02:14.489 --> 00:02:19.500
Film Courage: Do you think you didn’t screen
the people properly because they were so ready

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.660
to take the paycheck that you didn’t see
the commitment in them?

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:31.470
John: I think it was a combination of that
and just thinking that maybe I was trying

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:44.690
to...I think I took my vigilance off and not
qualifying people a level...I had a great

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:48.930
time working with all of those individuals
but I could have avoided a lot of problems

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:53.030
had I done better vetting of my cast and my
crew.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:57.950
That’s not to say I regret any of it because
it was a learning experience and we got a

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:03.090
film done and we did a really good job with
it and it impacted a lot of people but in

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:11.379
terms of my job as a producer, I stepped away
from certain things and just figured they

00:03:11.379 --> 00:03:15.250
were going to take care of themselves because
I had more money, that was a mistake I made.

